# Lean Terminal

A conviction-based swing trading terminal that translates 18 mathematical methods into one sentence a driver would understand.

> "I had no coding experience when I started thinking about this. About six months later, this terminal runs my real money."

---

## The Story

Late August 2025. I screenshot my portfolio and ask, "how can I improve this?" I have no programming background, no formal finance training, no quantitative methods education. What I have is an opinion that markets have patterns, and a stubborn belief I can find them.

Six months and a dozen build sessions later, this is what exists:

- **7,777 lines** of self-contained HTML running in any browser
- **18 mathematical methods** spanning information theory, dynamical systems, stochastic processes, and signal analysis
- **6 tabs** for analysis, comparison, regime detection, and historical backtest
- **Live deployment** at [nomnomsoes.github.io/lean-terminal](https://nomnomsoes.github.io/lean-terminal)
- **Real capital** deployed daily through this system

This is an AI-assisted project. Every concept, architecture decision, signal design, and product call is mine. The implementation was built collaboratively. In 2026 this is industry standard — what matters is whether you understand what you built and whether it works.

---

## Timeline

The project evolved through distinct phases:

- **Aug 2025** — Began screenshot-based portfolio research. Asking "how do I improve this?"
- **Oct 2025** — Named the campaign approach "Lean." Started thinking systematically.
- **Nov 2025** — Refined to "Lean 2.0" with risk-tier framework and disciplined sizing.
- **Feb 2026** — First "Lean Terminal" concept as an iPhone-readable app.
- **Mar 2026** — Heavy build phase. 600+ message sessions wiring the engine.
- **Apr 2026** — Formal L sessions begin (L1 on April 10). System goes live.
- **Today** — L12. 15 logged trades, 66 shipped features, 7,777 lines.

The "8-month journey" includes everything from first portfolio screenshot to today. The actual coded Lean Terminal as we know it took form over the past several months. The formal operator phase — where I trade based on the system's signals — has only been running about three weeks.

---

## The Approach

Most trading dashboards show you data and ask you to interpret it. This one shows you data, runs eighteen interpretations of it, then synthesizes those interpretations into a single actionable sentence.

The most distinctive feature is the **bus analogy** — translating 18 distinct mathematical methods into a single ten-state UX layer:

```
🚌 BUS AT THE STOP — HOP ON NOW
   8 EMA just crossed above 20 EMA today

🚌 on the bus — stay seated
   next stop around $204.30 (2.3% from 20 EMA)

🚌 next stop — get ready
   8 EMA slipping below 20, exit setup forming

🚌 GET OFF — HOP OFF NOW
   8 EMA just crossed below 20 EMA today
```

This isn't gimmickry. It's a translation layer between mathematical sophistication and actionable clarity. Behind the bus state is the full mathematical stack — but on the surface, it's a sentence anyone can understand.

---

## The Math

The terminal runs eighteen methods on every ticker click:

**Trend & Momentum**
- 8 / 20 / 50 EMA stack with crossover detection
- Adaptive trailing stop (EMA-based)
- Runner strategy (75% exit, 25% hold)

**Cycles & Frequency**
- Fourier cycle detection
- Cycle position tracking (0–100% phase)
- Signal decay half-life

**Stochastic Processes**
- Markov state probabilities (UP / FLAT / DOWN with continuation rates)
- Fokker-Planck probability density
- Monte Carlo path projections

**Information Theory**
- Shannon entropy (noise score)
- Lyapunov exponent (chaos vs stability)
- Confidence-weighted ensemble scoring

**Risk & Stress**
- Heat equation diffusion (cross-asset stress map)
- Pearson correlation (portfolio X-ray)
- Fatigue scoring (cumulative stress)
- Drawdown circuit breaker (10% peak-to-trough)

**Composite**
- Bus state classifier (10 states)
- Lens 5-layer regime overlay
- Mini lens compact verdict
- Per-ticker signal trust scoring

---

## The Six Tabs

**Terminal** — Daily decision dashboard. Today's moves, action engine, system metrics, top bull / top bear / highest reaction.

**DNA Lab** — Deep analysis on individual tickers. Full DNA panel, candlestick charts, history breakdown.

**Cheat Sheet** — Quick reference for what every score means and how to read every signal.

**Monte / Markov** — Probabilistic modeling. Monte Carlo path projections, Markov state flow grid, transition matrices.

**Race Card** — Comparative ranking with full math context. Used for "which of these should I trade?" decisions.

**Lens** — 5-layer regime overlay. Stock layer, family layer, market layer, calendar layer, anomaly layer. The verdict.

---

## The Baseball Card

Click any ticker on any tab and a baseball card opens — a single screen showing every number that matters:

- Holding status, P&L, theme classification, ranking
- Verdict line (synthesized natural language)
- Bus state (current action — hop on, stay seated, hop off)
- Signal strength, signal trust, system call, position size
- Direction probabilities (Markov: % up / flat / down)
- 8 / 20 / 50 EMA stack with current price relationship
- EMA stack state classifier (full bull / fading / weak / full bear / mixed)
- Adaptive stop level
- Noise, fatigue, runner allocation
- Mini lens (5 dots — stock / family / market / calendar / anomaly)
- "Does this fit?" overlap analysis for non-held tickers
- Buttons: compare, lens, full DNA, race card, close

No scrolling. No menus. No tabs to navigate. Click a ticker, get the full picture.

---

## The Stack

- **Frontend:** Single-file HTML, no build step, no framework
- **Data layer:** Google Sheets CSV publish feeds (read-only)
- **Live data:** Alpaca market data API
- **Hosting:** GitHub Pages (free, fast, version-controlled)
- **State:** localStorage for user preferences and settings
- **Math:** Pure vanilla JavaScript, no external libraries

This is intentional. The system can be deployed anywhere, runs without a backend, has zero dependencies, and survives indefinitely. The entire trading system is one HTML file.

---

## Live Operation

The terminal connects to:

- **Google Sheets** — for ranking, holdings, price history, Markov current state, and macro indicators
- **Alpaca** — for live price feeds and execution (paper / live toggle)
- **Robinhood** — for manual trade execution

The flow:

1. Google Sheet computes scores, signals, and probabilities (server side via formulas)
2. Terminal pulls CSV feeds, runs 18 math methods locally
3. User reviews baseball card and bus state
4. Trade executes through Robinhood (manual) or Alpaca (paper)
5. Result logs to TRADE_LOG, BEHAVIOR_LOG, and HISTORY tabs

---

## The Discipline

This system is operated by rules:

- **Daily routine:** open terminal at 4:15 PM PT, log portfolio value to HISTORY, log any trades to TRADE_LOG, log any overrides to BEHAVIOR_LOG.
- **Circuit breaker:** if portfolio drops 10% from peak, sell all and wait 14 days. Non-negotiable.
- **Bench days are valid:** if signals are noisy, do nothing. Logged just like trade days.
- **Behavior log is mandatory:** every system override gets a written reason. No exceptions.

The discipline is the system. The math is just the input.

